    "answer": {
      "short": "$n$ is prime if and only if $(n-1)!\\equiv-1\\pmod n$.\n\nFor prime $n$, pair each residue with its inverse; for composite $n$, a proper divisor prevents $(n-1)!+1$ from being divisible by $n$.",
      "worked": "Suppose $n$ is prime. If $n=2$, then $(n-1)!=1\\equiv-1\\pmod2$.\n\nNow suppose $n>2$. Every integer from $1$ to $n-1$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$. A residue is its own inverse precisely when $x^2\\equiv1\\pmod n$. Since\n$$x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1),$$\nand $n$ is prime, $n$ divides $x-1$ or $x+1$. Thus the only self-inverse residues are $1$ and $n-1$. Pair all remaining residues with their distinct inverses; each pair has product congruent to $1$. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}(n-1)!&\\equiv1\\cdot(n-1)\\pmod n\\\\(n-1)!&\\equiv-1\\pmod n.\\end{align*}$$\nConversely, suppose $n$ is composite. Choose a divisor $d$ with $1<d<n$. Since $d$ occurs among the factors of $(n-1)!$, $d\\mid(n-1)!$, so $d\\nmid((n-1)!+1)$. But if $(n-1)!\\equiv-1\\pmod n$, then $n\\mid((n-1)!+1)$, and hence $d\\mid((n-1)!+1)$, a contradiction. Therefore $n$ is prime.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
